Input: 
fruits = [1,2,3,2,2]
Output: 
4
Explanation: 
We can pick from trees [2,3,2,2].
If we had started at the first tree, we would only pick from trees [1,2]

Steps:
We are going to use two pointers to menion start and start of the sliding window as start and end.
First we are going to put values and its frequency inside a HashMap. 
If the value already exists, we increment the frequency by 1, else we add it as a new element and and initialise the key as 0+1.
If the size of the hashmap reaches 3, we decrement the frequency of the element in the start position.
After decrementing, if the frequency becomes 0, then we remove the pair from the hashmap.
Then we calculate currlen as the sum of end-start+1. To find the number of elements in the current window.
Then we return the max of the currlen and maxlen.

Code:
class Solution {
    public int totalFruit(int[] fruits) {
        int start=0,end=0;
        int maxlen=0;
        int n=fruits.length;
        HashMap<Integer,Integer> map=new HashMap<>();
        while(end<n){
            map.put(fruits[end],map.getOrDefault(fruits[end],0)+1);
            while(map.size()>=3){
                map.put(fruits[start],map.get(fruits[start])-1);
                if(map.get(fruits[start])==0){
                    map.remove(fruits[start]);
                }
                start++;
            }
            int currlen=end-start+1;
            maxlen=Math.max(currlen,maxlen);
            end++;
        }
        return maxlen;
    }
}
